@francesco_m.m To be fair, the original simplified snippet in the video used 255, so your point about integer division applied there. That’s exactly why I corrected it in the thread to 255.0.

The issue is that after the correction, you still confidently claimed i / 255.0 would perform integer division and “always be zero”, which is just not how C/C++ works. The .0 promotes the expression to floating point automatically. This is compiler-verifiable in about 10 seconds.

And honestly, this is an educational Instagram comment section meant to make gamma correction understandable and legible in code, not benchmark microcontrollers. Yes, LUTs and integer approximations are more optimal on constrained hardware. But, you literally said yourself that properly explaining those approaches is too much for an Insta comment, which is exactly why I used the clearer pow() example for the demo 🙌

@lakeramm23 For mashing inputs you’d just make the debounce window short enough that it filters the mechanical bounce, but still allows real human presses.

The bounce is usually only a few milliseconds, while even very fast button mashing is way slower than that. So you’re not blocking “real” inputs, you’re just ignoring the tiny fake transitions from the same press.

You can also debounce by waiting for the signal to be stable instead of using a fixed delay, but the core idea is the same: don’t count the contact bounce as separate presses.
